## Solution

We claim that $f_\varepsilon \to \pi\,\delta_0$ in $D'(\mathbb{R})$, i.e. for every test function $\varphi \in \mathcal{D}(\mathbb{R})$,

$$\lim_{\varepsilon \to 0^+} \langle f_\varepsilon, \varphi\rangle = \lim_{\varepsilon \to 0^+} \int_{-\infty}^{+\infty} \frac{\sin(\varepsilon x)}{x}\,\varphi(x)\,dx = \pi\,\varphi(0).$$

### Proof

Fix $\varphi \in C_c^\infty(\mathbb{R})$. Write

$$\int_{-\infty}^{+\infty} \frac{\sin(\varepsilon x)}{x}\,\varphi(x)\,dx = \int_{-\infty}^{+\infty} \frac{\sin(\varepsilon x)}{x}\bigl[\varphi(x)-\varphi(0)\bigr]\,dx \;+\; \varphi(0)\int_{-\infty}^{+\infty}\frac{\sin(\varepsilon x)}{x}\,dx.$$

**The second integral.** For $\varepsilon > 0$, the substitution $u = \varepsilon x$ gives

$$\int_{-\infty}^{+\infty}\frac{\sin(\varepsilon x)}{x}\,dx = \int_{-\infty}^{+\infty}\frac{\sin u}{u}\,du = \pi,$$

the classical Dirichlet integral value.

**The first integral.** Define

$$g(x) = \begin{cases} \dfrac{\varphi(x)-\varphi(0)}{x}, & x \neq 0,\\[6pt] \varphi'(0), & x = 0. \end{cases}$$

Since $\varphi$ is smooth, $\varphi(x) - \varphi(0) = x\,\varphi'(0) + \tfrac{x^2}{2}\varphi''(0) + \cdots$, so the singularity at $x=0$ is removable and $g \in C_c^\infty(\mathbb{R})$. The first integral becomes

$$\int_{-\infty}^{+\infty} \sin(\varepsilon x)\,g(x)\,dx.$$

This is the Fourier sine transform of $g$ evaluated at frequency $\varepsilon$. Since $g \in L^1(\mathbb{R})$ (it is continuous with compact support), the **Riemann–Lebesgue lemma** yields

$$\lim_{\varepsilon \to 0^+}\int_{-\infty}^{+\infty} \sin(\varepsilon x)\,g(x)\,dx = 0.$$

**Combining.** Therefore

$$\lim_{\varepsilon \to 0^+} \int_{-\infty}^{+\infty} \frac{\sin(\varepsilon x)}{x}\,\varphi(x)\,dx = 0 + \varphi(0)\cdot\pi = \pi\,\varphi(0) = \langle \pi\,\delta_0,\,\varphi\rangle.$$

Since this holds for every $\varphi \in \mathcal{D}(\mathbb{R})$,

$$\boxed{\lim_{\varepsilon\to 0^+} f_\varepsilon = \pi\,\delta_0 \quad \text{in } D'(\mathbb{R}).}$$

